---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="2D Art"
  description="The game has thousands of 2D images packed into a few hundred container files. They fall into a handful of kinds, and each kind has its own rule for how to render it."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: '2D Art' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Almost no 2D image in the game is stored as a finished picture. Instead,
    you get the ingredients — a sheet of tiles, a palette, and instructions
    for how to assemble them. To render an image you have to find those three
    things, match them up correctly, and run the composition.
  </p>

  <p>
    The big practical problem is that the palette for a given image often
    lives somewhere else. The next few pages cover the kinds of art and the
    rules for finding their palettes.
  </p>

  <div class="fg-cards">
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/2d-art/kinds-of-art/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>The kinds of art</h3>
      <p>Item icons, magic glyphs, NPC portraits, background screens, UI sprites. What each kind is for, where it lives, and roughly how many of each exist.</p>
    </a>
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/2d-art/palettes/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>Finding the right palette</h3>
      <p>The five-rule hierarchy for matching a tile sheet to its colors. Why item icons "look broken" until you find the palette inside <code>global.lzs</code>.</p>
    </a>
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/2d-art/composition-recipes/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>Composition recipes</h3>
      <p>How a shop screen gets assembled at runtime from a handful of sprite chips and an XY table. The buyitem screen, fully worked.</p>
    </a>
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/2d-art/magazines/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>The magazine system</h3>
      <p>63 in-game magazine pages plus an index, a frame, and a trophy table. Every Japanese word is baked into the pixels — translating them means redrawing them.</p>
    </a>
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/2d-art/stamps/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>NPC stamps</h3>
      <p>140 character stamps used in mail and bio sheets, all sharing a single 512-byte palette. Plus the stamp-picker UI frame.</p>
    </a>
  </div>

  <h2>How many images are there?</h2>
  <p>
    We catalogued 4,337 sets of tile graphics across the game. Each set has
    siblings (a palette, sometimes an OAM sprite definition, sometimes a
    background tilemap) telling us how to render it. The breakdown:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Kind of art</th><th>Count</th><th>What it is</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Background screens</td>
        <td>1,100</td>
        <td>Full 256×192 backgrounds assembled from a tilemap. Shop interiors, menus, title art.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Sprite cells</td>
        <td>704</td>
        <td>Animated or composite sprites assembled from many small "OBJ" chips. Character poses, UI widgets.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Tile banks</td>
        <td>296</td>
        <td>Loose tile sheets without explicit composition. Often atlases.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Orphans (palette comes from elsewhere)</td>
        <td>2,237</td>
        <td>Item icons, magic glyphs, and other sets whose palette is pinned somewhere outside the container.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Of the 2,237 orphan tile sheets, we resolved 1,637 by finding their
    external palettes (656 item icons, 823 magic glyphs, plus shared-palette
    containers). The remaining 600 are mostly in containers like
    <code>signboard.lzs</code> and the fashion-design files, where the
    pairing between tile sheet and palette isn't yet known.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
